       "code": "logs = [(True, True)] * 11 + [(True, False)] * 4 + [(False, False)] * 4 + [(False, True)] * 1     # (the right passage was retrieved, the answer was right)\nretr_miss = sum(1 for r, a in logs if not r and not a)\ngen_error = sum(1 for r, a in logs if r and not a)\nlucky = sum(1 for r, a in logs if not r and a)\nprint(len(logs), \"questions;\", sum(a for _, a in logs), \"answered correctly\")\nprint(\"failures from retrieval:\", retr_miss, \"| from generation:\", gen_error, \"| right without the passage:\", lucky)",
       "say": "**Where did the failures come from?** For 20 logged questions (a stand-in for a real log) record two facts: was the right passage retrieved, and was the answer right. Of the 8 wrong answers, **4 were retrieval failures** (the passage was never fetched: improve the search, chunking or the embedding) and **4 were generation failures** (the passage was there and the model still got it wrong: improve the prompt, the model or the instructions to quote the source); one correct answer came without the right passage (luck, or the model's own memory: not to be counted on). End-to-end accuracy of 12 of 20 is the same number in two very different situations; **splitting the failures tells you what to fix first**.",
