       "code": "margin = np.abs(s1)\nbins = [0, 0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 10]\nfor lo, hi in zip(bins[:-1], bins[1:]):\n    m = (margin >= lo) & (margin < hi)\n    print(f\"score margin {lo:4.2f} to {min(hi, 9):4.2f}: {int(m.sum()):3d} pairs, agreement {(pref_first[m] == human_A[m]).mean():.2f}\")",
       "say": "**Does it know when it is unsure?** The judge's raw score has a size: close to 0 means a near-tie, far from 0 a clear call. Grouping the pairs by that margin shows how its accuracy grows with it: near ties are close to a coin flip (54% agreement with humans on the 54 closest pairs), clear calls (margin above 1) agree with humans 87% of the time. A real judge can be asked for a confidence or a score for the same reason. So you can **trust the confident verdicts and send the uncertain ones to a human**, which is how most teams put a judge to work: the people spend their time where the judge cannot help.",
